; A target form's member set is collected *once*, keyed by the canonical
; target name and built first-wins in plugin × value-kind order. So a
; second `(cross-ref …)` naming the same target contributes nothing to
; the registry — and when the two disagree about how the set is built,
; the loser's references end up checked against names its own
; declaration had no part in producing.
;
; Before the provider route the worst two declarations could differ on
; was a key. Now they can differ on the *route*, which is what makes the
; collapse worth a diagnostic: `shader-name` reads a symbol off each
; `(shader …)`, `uniform-name` would have run `lines` over the same
; form's `:src` string, and only the first of those happens.
;
; `.warning`, not `.err`: the schema still loads and every reference is
; still checked — just not the way the second kind declared.
(plugin :name probe :version "1.0.0" :sjon "1.2"
  (cross-ref-provider :name lines
    :description "One name per non-empty line.")

  (form :name shader
    (key :name name :type symbol :optional false)
    (key :name src :type string :optional false))

  ; Declared first, so this one builds the registry: the members of
  ; `probe/shader` are the symbols under `:name`.
  (value-kind :name shader-name
    :underlying symbol
    :cross-ref (cross-ref :target shader))

  ; Declared second on the same target, by the other route. Inert.
  (value-kind :name uniform-name
    :underlying symbol
    :cross-ref (cross-ref :target shader :provider lines)))

nil
